      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Ranked Them</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested the 2026 versions of these treadmills. The ranking is built from
            Sole's published specifications and warranty terms, independent review coverage and,
            where one existed, a retailer listing, all checked on 13 October 2026.
          </p>
          <p>
            We started with Sole's own treadmill category, which listed eight models, and read the
            specification table on each product page. We then compared every figure with
            independent reviews and roundups of the same model. Where Sole's current listing and an
            independent source agreed, the figure went into the spec line. Where they did not, it
            stayed out, and the card says which figure is in dispute and what each side says.
          </p>
          <p>
            On Sole that rule removes more than it does on most brands, for a specific reason: most
            of the range is marked as new for 2026, and the independent coverage — much of it
            updated this year — still describes the previous versions. Rather than pick one set of
            numbers, we have published only the figures that survived the change and flagged the
            rest. It makes some cards shorter than we would like, but every number on them is one
            you can rely on.
          </p>
          <p>
            The ranking itself weighs four things in order: how completely a model's figures are
            confirmed, because a buyer cannot weigh a specification nobody agrees on; the deck and
            user rating, because they decide who the machine suits; the price at which it is
            actually sold; and the console, last, because Sole's apps are free on every model and a
            screen is a convenience rather than a requirement.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="generations" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Why Sole Specifications Conflict</h2>
<p>
            Sole keeps model names for many years while the machine underneath changes. An F80
            bought a few years ago, an F80 described in a review updated this spring and an F80
            ordered from Sole today can have different motors, decks and consoles, and all three
            are simply called the F80. Retailer listings make it worse, because they often carry
            forward the specification block from whichever version they first listed.
          </p>
          <p>
            On 13 October 2026 the differences between Sole's 2026 listings and the most common
            figures elsewhere were these. The <strong>F80</strong>: Sole now lists a 3.25 HP motor
            and a 20 by 60-inch deck, where other sources give 3.5 HP and 22 by 60 inches. The
            <strong>F85</strong>: Sole lists 3.5 HP, a 350 lb rating and incline from zero, where
            other sources give 4.0 HP, 375 lb and decline to -6%. The <strong>F65</strong>: Sole lists
            a 20 by 60-inch deck, a 350 lb rating and an LED console, where other sources give 22 by
            60 inches, 325 lb and a 9-inch LCD. The <strong>F63</strong> is the exception: its deck,
            motor, speed, incline and rating match across sources, and only its console and machine
            weight have changed.
          </p>
          <p>
            Three practical rules follow. First, read the specification on Sole's current product
            page for the exact model, not a review, and save a copy of it with your order. Second,
            if you are buying from a retailer rather than from Sole, ask which model year the unit
            is and get the motor rating, deck size and user rating in writing. Third, if a
            discounted listing quotes figures that look better than Sole's own — a wider deck, a
            stronger motor, decline — assume it may be older stock and ask before you assume it is
            a bargain.
          </p>
          <p>
            We would rather show a shorter spec line than a wrong one. If Sole confirms a single
            set of figures for a given model year, we will add them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="no-subscription" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. No Subscription, and What the Screens Actually Do</h2>
<p>
            The clearest difference between Sole and the iFIT brands is that nothing on a Sole
            treadmill depends on a paid membership. Sole's product pages describe its app as free
            with no monthly subscription, and every model's console controls speed, incline and the
            built-in programs on its own.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>LED consoles on the F50, F63 and F65.</strong> The 2026 versions use a white LED
            display, with dial-style speed and incline controls on the F50 and F63. You bring your
            own device for video or apps. Nothing here ages the way a touchscreen does,
            and nothing stops working when a membership lapses, because there is none.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Touchscreens on the F80, F83 and F85.</strong> These add a 10.1-inch or
            15.6-inch touchscreen with Wi-Fi, Bluetooth and streaming apps. The screen is a
            convenience rather than the point of the machine: it runs entertainment and Sole's own
            app, and nothing on Sole's listings suggests it gates any speed, incline or program
            feature that the LED models lack. That makes the extra cost easier to judge — you are paying for a built-in screen,
            not for access to the treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>How this compares with ProForm and NordicTrack.</strong> On an iFIT machine,
            much of the screen's value depends on a membership that renews for as long as you want
            the classes, and ProForm requires an iFIT account to activate its machines at all. Our
            <a href="/best-proform-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">ProForm roundup</a>
            sets out how much of each ProForm depends on it. If avoiding subscriptions is the
            priority, our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills without subscriptions</a>
            roundup compares Sole with other brands built the same way.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. The Warranty Structure, Read by Component</h2>
<p>
            Sole's warranty is its strongest selling point, and it is worth reading by component
            rather than by the headline.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Frame and motor: lifetime on every folding model.</strong> Sole's current
            listings give lifetime cover on the frame and motor for the F50, F63, F65, F80, F83 and
            F85, and every independent source we checked agrees. That is the single most important
            difference from the iFIT brands: ProForm covers its motors for two years at most, and
            NordicTrack only covers the motor for life on its more expensive machines. The C80 has
            no drive motor, and its frame is covered for life.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Deck and electronics: two or three years.</strong> Sole's current listings give
            two years on the deck and electronics for the F63 and F65, and three years for the F80
            and F85. Independent sources quote different terms for earlier versions, so treat these
            as Sole's 2026 figures and confirm them for your unit.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Wear parts and labour: short.</strong> Sole's current listings give one year on
            wear items and 90 days on labour for the F63, F65, F80, F85 and C80. Short labour cover
            means a technician's visit after the first three months is usually at your expense,
            even when the part is covered. That is the weak point in an otherwise long warranty,
            and it is worth knowing before you compare it with a brand that offers a year of labour.
          </p>
          <p>
            Warranty terms vary by retailer and change between model years. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a>
            explains how to read component terms and what a lifetime motor warranty does and does
            not cover in practice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="not-ranked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. The Sole Models We Did Not Rank, and Which to Skip</h2>
<p>
            Three of Sole's current treadmills are not in the cards above, and several older names
            are still on sale elsewhere. Here is why.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The F50.</strong> New for 2026 and the cheapest Sole, with a 20 by 55-inch deck,
            a 2.0 HP motor and a 10 mph top speed on Sole's listing. We found no independent coverage
            of this version to cross-check those figures against, so we have not ranked it. On Sole's
            own numbers it is a walking machine; anyone who will jog regularly should look at the
            F63, which has a 60-inch deck and 12 mph for about $200 more.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The F83.</strong> Also new for 2026, listed by Sole with a 3.5 HP motor, a 22 by
            60-inch deck and a 10.1-inch touchscreen. We found no independent review of this version,
            and an older, different machine sold as the F83 — with a 9-inch LCD and different motor
            and capacity figures — still appears in reseller listings. Until independent coverage of the
            2026 model exists, we have left it out; if you buy one, check the console in the photos
            and confirm the model year.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The TT8.</strong> Sole's non-folding, light-commercial model was listed as sold
            out on Sole's site on the day we checked. Sole lists a 4.0 HP motor, a 22 by 60-inch deck,
            incline from -6% to 15% and a 400 lb rating, and independent sources agree on those. If
            it comes back into stock, it is the Sole with decline and the highest user rating.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Older stock and other names.</strong> Previous-generation F65s, F80s and F85s are
            still listed by some retailers, often with the older specification blocks. They are not
            bad machines, but you are buying a different specification from the one on Sole's site
            today, so get the model year and figures in writing. The ST90 and S77, which appear in
            some roundups, were not on Sole's US treadmill page when we checked. And as with any
            brand, a model at full price is rarely the best deal: every in-stock Sole on the site was
            discounted by $100 to $200 on the day we checked.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="owning-one" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Delivery, Setup and the First Month</h2>
<p>
            A Sole is a heavy machine, and a few practical points are worth knowing before one
            arrives.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Expect a heavy box.</strong> Sole lists the 2026 F63 at 187 lb, the F80 at 218 lb
            and the F85 at 243 lb before packaging, and the C80 at 261 lb. Measure the route from the
            door to the room, including stair turns, and decide early whether to pay for in-room
            delivery or assembly.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Plan the space.</strong> Sole lists in-use footprints of about 75 by 34 inches
            for the F63 and F80, 77 by 36 inches for the F65 and 78 by 38 inches for the F85. Leave
            clearance behind the deck, and add your height, the step-up, the extra lift at 15%
            incline and a margin for the bounce of running, and check the total against the ceiling.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Give it its own power.</strong> Motorised treadmills draw a lot of current on
            start-up and under load, and manuals generally specify a dedicated grounded circuit.
            Our
            <a href="/treadmill-extension-cord/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">extension cord and circuit guide</a>
            explains why a shared power strip causes faults that look like a failing machine. The
            C80 needs no power for the belt at all.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Record the specification on day one.</strong> Given how much Sole's figures have
            changed between generations, note the model year and serial number from the frame
            label, and keep the specification page you ordered from. If a warranty claim ever turns
            on which version you own, that record settles it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Use the first weeks properly.</strong> Run the machine at the speeds and inclines
            you plan to use, not a cautious ten minutes. Listen for roller or motor noise, watch
            whether the belt drifts, and check that the console's speed matches reality. A fault found
            early is a straightforward return; the same fault found after the labour cover ends can
            cost you a service call.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Who Should Buy a Different Brand</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If you want trainer-led classes on the screen.</strong> Sole's touchscreens run
            streaming apps and Sole's free app, not a class library that controls the machine for
            you. If that is the point, the iFIT brands are built around it — see our
            <a href="/best-nordictrack-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack roundup</a>
            and <a href="/best-proform-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">ProForm roundup</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you need decline today.</strong> The only current Sole with decline on its
            listing is the TT8, which was sold out when we checked. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-with-decline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">decline treadmill roundup</a>
            covers machines you can buy now.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you weigh more than about 325 lb.</strong> The folding Soles are rated to 350
            lb at most on Sole's current figures, and a rating is a ceiling rather than a
            comfortable operating point. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">high-capacity guide</a>
            covers machines with more margin.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you want a curved trainer and the C80's warranty worries you.</strong> Our
            <a href="/best-curved-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">curved treadmill roundup</a>
            compares it with machines that carry longer parts cover.
          </p>
        </div>

